body:
**Overflow disconnect drops every already-buffered frame instead of delivering it.**

Repro: subscribe with `follow=true`, reach `caught_up`, then append `FOLLOW_QUEUE_LIMIT + 1` (1025) records in one burst and let the tailer wake fire (mirrors the PR's own "1025th queued envelope" test at `follow.service.test.ts:292`). The very first `gen.next()` call after the burst resolves to `{ done: true, value: undefined }` — zero frames are ever yielded to the client, even though `enqueue()` (lines 73–83) successfully queued 1024 legitimate envelopes before the 1025th failed.

Why: `scan()` (lines 88–121) enqueues emissions synchronously until `enqueue` returns `false` for the 1025th, setting `overflowed = true` and breaking out — the queue at that point holds up to 1024 valid, in-bound frames. Control returns to the main loop via `wakeLoop()`, but the loop's very first check is `if (overflowed) return;` (line 131), which fires *before* the existing `queue.shift()` drain logic ever runs. The 1024 buffered frames are discarded, not sent.

This contradicts both the PR description ("the 1025th disconnects the subscriber \[so 1..1024 implicitly went out]") and the `enqueue` comment itself ("Nothing queued is owed to it" — referring to the 1025th record specifically, implying the prior 1024 *are* owed). The existing test at line 312 (`expect((await pending).done).toBe(true)`) only asserts the generator eventually finishes; it never asserts any frame was actually yielded before that, so it passes whether or not the buffered frames are delivered — it doesn't catch this.

Fix: drain `queue` fully before honoring `overflowed`, e.g. swap the order so the loop only returns on `overflowed` once `queue.shift()` yields `undefined`:
```ts
for (;;) {
  const next = queue.shift();
  if (next !== undefined) {
    yield next;
    continue;
  }
  if (overflowed || closing) return;
  ...
}
```

